            <ArticleTitle>Integrative approach focusing on acupuncture in the treatment of chronic complex regional pain syndrome.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>67-70</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1089/acm.2010.0088</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Chronic complex regional pain syndrome (CRPS) is a chronic pain condition that leads to sympathetic nervous system involvement and trophic changes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This study describes the use of acupuncture in a case study of CRPS.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN, SETTING, AND PATIENT" NlmCategory="METHODS">This is a single case report of a 34-year-old patient diagnosed with CRPS.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Acupuncture treatment including acupoints along the Gallbladder, Liver, Spleen, Heart, and Kidney meridians. Self-treatment plan included a laser acupuncture pen device and disposable press needles.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Beck Depression Inventory (BDI), McGill Pain Questionnaire, and Sheehan Disability Scale (SDS).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The Patient reported a decrease in pain levels, depression, and an improved quality of life. Pretreatment SDS score of 17, a 12 on the BDI, and a 67 on the McGill Pain Questionnaire. Post-treatment SDS decreased to 4, her BDI went to 0, and her McGill Pain Questionnaire decreased to a 10.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">More research is needed and case studies performed to support our findings.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
